# =============================================================================
# Configuration reference - EVERY environment variable referenced-automation-ui-api reads, or that the shared
# packages (utils, api, ui, sap) read on its behalf. The shared keys are documented in full in
# https://github.com/achaljoshi/referenced-automation-utils/blob/master/.env.example - the wording here is the same.
# This file is documentation: nothing loads it. Copy the lines you need into
#
#   .env.<name>          committed, NO secrets      (.env.qa, .env.stage, .env.dev)
#   .env.<name>.local    git-ignored, secrets only  (template: .env.local.example)
#
# and pick the file with the ENV variable (default qa). Load order, later wins:
#   .env -> .env.<ENV> -> .env.<ENV>.local -> the real environment (shell / CI variables).
# The secret file is named after the environment: ENV=qa reads .env.qa.local, NOT .env.local.
#
# Every variable below is documented the same way, on the lines above it:
#   what it is for / Read from / Values / Default | Required | Secret / Example
# "Read from" matters: a variable marked "real environment only" is read straight from
# process.env, so a line for it in a .env file has NO effect - set it in the shell or in CI:
#   bash / zsh : LOG_LEVEL=debug npx playwright test
#   PowerShell : $env:LOG_LEVEL = "debug"; npx playwright test      (undo: Remove-Item Env:LOG_LEVEL)
#   cmd.exe    : set LOG_LEVEL=debug && npx playwright test
#
# TIP - quoting values (dotenv rules):
#   * A value that contains # (starts a comment: PASSWORD=pa#ss is read as "pa"), $, quotes, or leading/trailing
#     spaces must be wrapped in double quotes:  REMOTE_RDP_PASSWORD="pa#ss $word 'x'"
#   * Inside double quotes \n becomes a line break, so a Windows value with backslashes (DOMAIN\new-user,
#     C:\new\dir) is safer unquoted or in single quotes:  REMOTE_RDP_USER='DOMAIN\new-user'
#   * A value that itself contains a double quote goes in single quotes:  NOTE='say "hi"'
#   * A key with an empty value (KEY=) is set to the empty string, which is not the same as not set.
#
# Secrets (Secret: YES) belong in .env.<name>.local or the CI secret store - never in a committed file:
#   cp .env.local.example .env.qa.local          (PowerShell: Copy-Item .env.local.example .env.qa.local)
#
# This repo's own tests start an in-process sample server and the SAP simulator, so they run with NONE of these keys set;
# the keys below are what a real hybrid UI + API + SAP project built the same way sets. Keys the shared packages read for
# features this repo does not use (mainframe 3270, SMTP / SFTP, the other REMOTE_* keys, the SAP_GUI_* tuning keys) are
# in the utils and sap .env.example files, not repeated here.
#
# Variable lines below are commented out; remove the leading "# " to use one.
# Tests that check this file: tests/envDocs.spec.ts (every variable the code reads is here, and nothing else).
# =============================================================================


# ---- Environment selection ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Which environment to run against = which .env.<name> files are loaded and what is shown as "env" in the report.
# Read from: the real environment only (it chooses the file, so a line ENV=... inside a file is ignored).
# Values: any name that has a .env.<name> file here: qa | stage | dev | your own   (a name with no file just loads no file)
# Default: qa | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: ENV=stage
# ENV=qa

# Fallback name for ENV, for machines where another tool already owns ENV. ENV wins when both are set.
# Read from: the real environment only
# Values: same as ENV
# Default: unset (then qa) | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: ENVIRONMENT=stage
# ENVIRONMENT=qa


# ---- Target URLs (a real landscape; the sample server and the SAP simulator need neither) -------------------------

# Base URL of the web application. playwright.config.ts passes BASE_URL as baseUrlKey, so it becomes Playwright's baseURL and page.goto('/login') works.
# Note: the api package's apiClient fixture calls Playwright's baseURL when it is set and API_BASE_URL only when BASE_URL is empty, so with both set apiClient calls BASE_URL; when the API lives on another host build the client yourself: new ApiClient(request, env.get('API_BASE_URL')).
# Read from: .env files or the real environment (read by createPlaywrightConfig; this repo's own tests start the sample server and ignore it)
# Values: an absolute URL with scheme, e.g. https://qa.example.com
# Default: unset | Required: no for the config itself; yes for any test that opens a relative URL | Secret: no
# Example: BASE_URL=https://qa.example.com
# BASE_URL=https://qa.example.com

# Base URL of the API under test: what the api package's apiClient / apiFor fixtures call when BASE_URL is empty, and the host the api steps in the Gherkin features use.
# Read from: .env files or the real environment (read by the api package, relative to the .env files next to playwright.config.ts)
# Values: an absolute URL with scheme, e.g. https://api.qa.example.com
# Default: unset | Required: no for the config itself; yes for any test that calls a relative path against a real API | Secret: no
# Example: API_BASE_URL=https://api.qa.example.com
# API_BASE_URL=https://api.qa.example.com


# ---- Browser and run selection (createPlaywrightConfig) ----------------------------------------------------------

# Which browser(s) to run. An unknown value fails at start-up with the list of valid names.
# Read from: .env files or the real environment
# Values: chromium | firefox | webkit | msedge | msedge-beta | msedge-dev | chrome | chrome-beta | chrome-dev | all (the three engines) | all-devices (the mobile devices)
# Default: chromium | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: BROWSER=firefox
# BROWSER=chromium

# Emulate one Playwright device instead of a desktop browser; wins over BROWSER. An unknown name fails at start-up.
# Read from: .env files or the real environment
# Values: any name in Playwright's device list, e.g. "iPhone 15", "Pixel 7", "iPad Pro 11" (quote names with spaces)
# Default: unset (then BROWSER decides) | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: DEVICE="iPhone 15"
# DEVICE=

# Run the browser without a window.
# Read from: .env files or the real environment
# Values: true | false (also 1/0, yes/no, on/off; anything else, such as "ture", is an error)
# Default: true | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: HEADLESS=false
# HEADLESS=true

# How many test files run at the same time.
# Read from: .env files or the real environment
# Values: a whole number >= 1, or a percentage of the logical cores such as 50%; a suite that passes maxWorkers (SAP: 1) is capped by it
# Default: unset (Playwright uses half of the logical cores) | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: WORKERS=4
# WORKERS=

# How often a failed test is run again before it counts as failed (a test that passes on a retry is reported as flaky).
# Read from: .env files or the real environment
# Values: a whole number >= 0
# Default: 0 on a developer machine; 1 when the CI variable is set (the repo's retriesOnCi option can change that) | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: RETRIES=2
# RETRIES=0

# Stop the whole run after this many failed tests.
# Read from: .env files or the real environment
# Values: a whole number >= 0 (0 = never stop early)
# Default: 0 | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: MAX_FAILURES=5
# MAX_FAILURES=0

# Slow every browser action down so a person can watch (debugging only).
# Read from: .env files or the real environment
# Values: milliseconds, a whole number >= 0 (0 = off)
# Default: 0 | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: SLOW_MO_MS=250
# SLOW_MO_MS=0

# Width of the browser window for desktop projects.
# Read from: .env files or the real environment
# Values: pixels, a whole number
# Default: 1280 | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: VIEWPORT_WIDTH=1920
# VIEWPORT_WIDTH=1280

# Height of the browser window for desktop projects.
# Read from: .env files or the real environment
# Values: pixels, a whole number
# Default: 800 | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: VIEWPORT_HEIGHT=1080
# VIEWPORT_HEIGHT=800

# Browser language (navigator.language, Accept-Language, date and number formats).
# Read from: .env files or the real environment
# Values: a BCP 47 tag such as en-GB, de-DE, fr-CA
# Default: unset (the browser's own) | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: LOCALE=en-GB
# LOCALE=

# Browser time zone, so date assertions do not depend on where the test machine is.
# Read from: .env files or the real environment
# Values: an IANA zone name such as Europe/London, America/New_York, UTC
# Default: unset (the machine's own) | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: TIMEZONE_ID=Europe/London
# TIMEZONE_ID=

# Emulated prefers-color-scheme.
# Read from: .env files or the real environment
# Values: light | dark | no-preference
# Default: unset (the browser's own) | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: COLOR_SCHEME=dark
# COLOR_SCHEME=

# Accept invalid or self-signed HTTPS certificates (test environments only).
# Read from: .env files or the real environment
# Values: true | false (also 1/0, yes/no, on/off)
# Default: false | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: IGNORE_HTTPS_ERRORS=true
# IGNORE_HTTPS_ERRORS=false

# The attribute getByTestId() looks at.
# Read from: .env files or the real environment
# Values: an HTML attribute name, e.g. data-testid, data-test, data-qa
# Default: data-testid | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: TEST_ID_ATTRIBUTE=data-test
# TEST_ID_ATTRIBUTE=data-testid


# ---- Timeouts, all in milliseconds (createPlaywrightConfig) ------------------------------------------------------

# How long one whole test may take.
# Read from: .env files or the real environment
# Values: milliseconds, a whole number
# Default: 30000 (a repo can change the default through the testTimeoutMs option) | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: TEST_TIMEOUT_MS=60000
# TEST_TIMEOUT_MS=30000

# How long a web-first assertion (expect(...)) waits for its condition.
# Read from: .env files or the real environment
# Values: milliseconds, a whole number
# Default: 10000 | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: EXPECT_TIMEOUT_MS=15000
# EXPECT_TIMEOUT_MS=10000

# How long one action (click, fill, ...) waits for its element.
# Read from: .env files or the real environment
# Values: milliseconds, a whole number
# Default: 15000 | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: ACTION_TIMEOUT_MS=20000
# ACTION_TIMEOUT_MS=15000

# How long a navigation (page.goto, link clicks) waits.
# Read from: .env files or the real environment
# Values: milliseconds, a whole number
# Default: 30000 | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: NAVIGATION_TIMEOUT_MS=60000
# NAVIGATION_TIMEOUT_MS=30000

# How long the whole run may take before Playwright stops it.
# Read from: .env files or the real environment
# Values: milliseconds, a whole number (0 = no limit)
# Default: 0 | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: GLOBAL_TIMEOUT_MS=3600000
# GLOBAL_TIMEOUT_MS=0


# ---- Logging (logger/logger.ts in utils) -------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The committed .env.dev / .env.qa / .env.stage set LOG_LEVEL, but the logger does not read .env files, so that line has no effect there.

# Minimum level written to the console and the log file.
# Read from: the real environment only (the logger reads process.env once, when it is first imported; .env files are not read)
# Values: error | warn | info | http | verbose | debug | silly
# Default: info | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: LOG_LEVEL=debug
# LOG_LEVEL=info

# Also write the JSON-lines log file (a CI artifact). If the file cannot be created the logger carries on with the console only.
# Read from: the real environment only
# Values: true | false (only the text "false", any case, turns it off)
# Default: true | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: LOG_TO_FILE=false
# LOG_TO_FILE=true

# Where the log file is written.
# Read from: the real environment only
# Values: a path, relative to the folder tests start in or absolute
# Default: logs/automation.log | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: LOG_FILE=logs/run-42.log
# LOG_FILE=logs/automation.log

# One log file per Playwright worker (logs/automation-w2.log) instead of every worker appending to one file.
# Read from: the real environment only
# Values: true | false (only the text "true", any case, turns it on)
# Default: false | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: LOG_FILE_PER_WORKER=true
# LOG_FILE_PER_WORKER=false

# Switch coloured console output off. Colour is already off when CI is set or the output is not a terminal.
# Read from: the real environment only
# Values: any non-empty value turns colour off (the NO_COLOR convention)
# Default: unset | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: NO_COLOR=1
# NO_COLOR=

# Switch coloured console output on even in CI or when the output is piped.
# Read from: the real environment only
# Values: any non-empty value turns colour on (even 0 or false - it is the presence that counts)
# Default: unset | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: FORCE_COLOR=1
# FORCE_COLOR=


# ---- Test data -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Seeds the random-data generators (randomUtils, {{random:...}} placeholders) for every Playwright worker, so a failing
# run can be replayed with the same data. The seed in use is logged at start-up.
# Read from: the real environment only
# Values: an integer
# Default: unset (a different random seed every run) | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: RANDOM_SEED=12345
# RANDOM_SEED=


# ---- This repo: the sample server, its logins and the saved session (tests/support, .auth/) ---------------------------
# The sample server (tests/support/authServer.ts) is what the tests, the converted recordings and the Gherkin features log in to.
# .auth/ holds what the tests save between runs: session.key (the random signing key, created on first use) and user.json
# (the browser state saved by tests/auth.setup.ts, a credential). Both are git-ignored; delete the folder to start fresh.

# The key the sample server signs its session cookies with. A server with another key does not recognise a session the first one issued.
# Read from: the real environment only (tests/support/authServer.ts reads process.env directly, so a line in a .env file has NO effect - set it in the shell or in CI)
# Values: any text; a long random string
# Default: unset (then a random key is created once in .auth/session.key and shared by every sample server of this checkout) | Required: no | Secret: no - it signs the sample server's sessions only and protects nothing real; never reuse a real key for it
# Example: AUTH_SESSION_KEY=local-sample-key
# AUTH_SESSION_KEY=

# The password the converted API tests (tests/generated/api, from curl/auth.curl) send. The sample server only accepts the made-up login ada / secret.
# Read from: the real environment, or .env files for a real API - but tests/support/generatedTest.ts sets process.env.API_PASSWORD to "secret" when the real environment lacks it, and the real environment wins over .env files, so in this repo a .env.<name>.local line is overridden until you delete that line
# Values: any text; against a real API, the real password of the test user
# Default: secret (the sample server's made-up login) | Required: no for this repo's tests; yes for the converted API tests against a real API | Secret: YES - .env.<name>.local or a CI secret, never a committed file
# Example (in .env.qa.local): API_PASSWORD="<api-password>"
# API_PASSWORD=

# The password the Gherkin feature features/hybrid-login.feature sends as {{env:HYBRID_PASSWORD|secret}} when it logs in through the API.
# Read from: .env files or the real environment (the {{env:...}} placeholder of the feature)
# Values: any text; against a real system, the real password of the test user "ada"
# Default: secret (the sample server's made-up login) | Required: no for this repo's tests; yes for the feature against a real system | Secret: YES - .env.<name>.local or a CI secret, never a committed file
# Example (in .env.qa.local): HYBRID_PASSWORD="<hybrid-password>"
# HYBRID_PASSWORD=


# ---- SAP GUI (read by the sap package; every SAP_GUI_* key: https://github.com/achaljoshi/referenced-automation-sap/blob/master/.env.example) ----
# This repo runs SAP against the package's built-in simulator: the Gherkin features replace the transport with
# createSampleSapSystem() (features/steps/fixtures.ts) and pin sapGuiMode to 'attach' (the bdd project in playwright.config.ts).
# So on this machine the SAP_* keys below are not needed, and SAP_GUI_MODE has no effect until you remove that pin. For a real SAP GUI (Windows with SAP GUI installed) remove the overrides, then these keys apply.
# features/steps/fixtures.ts sets process.env.SAP_USER, SAP_PASSWORD and SAP_CLIENT to the simulator's made-up values when the REAL environment lacks them; the
# real environment wins over .env files, so for the Gherkin features a .env.<name>.local line is overridden until you delete those three lines.

# How the SAP GUI session is obtained.
# Read from: .env files or the real environment (the sap package; ignored here while sapGuiMode is pinned to attach)
# Values: attach (use the SAP Logon window already open) | open (open SAP_GUI_CONNECTION_NAME or SAP_GUI_CONNECTION_STRING)
# Default: attach | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: SAP_GUI_MODE=open
# SAP_GUI_MODE=attach

# Run against the sap package's built-in fake SAP instead of a real one (any operating system).
# Read from: .env files or the real environment (the sap package)
# Values: true | false (also 1/0, yes/no, on/off)
# Default: false | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: SAP_GUI_SIMULATOR=true
# SAP_GUI_SIMULATOR=false

# The SAP Logon entry to open, exactly as listed in SAP Logon (only for SAP_GUI_MODE=open).
# Read from: .env files or the real environment (the sap package)
# Values: the entry's name, e.g. "QA1 - ERP Quality" (quote names with spaces)
# Default: unset | Required: yes with SAP_GUI_MODE=open unless SAP_GUI_CONNECTION_STRING is set | Secret: no
# Example: SAP_GUI_CONNECTION_NAME="QA1 - ERP Quality"
# SAP_GUI_CONNECTION_NAME=

# Open a system by connection string instead of a SAP Logon entry (only for SAP_GUI_MODE=open).
# Read from: .env files or the real environment (the sap package)
# Values: a SAP GUI connection string (quote it: it contains spaces and = signs)
# Default: unset | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: SAP_GUI_CONNECTION_STRING="/H/sap.example.internal/S/3200"
# SAP_GUI_CONNECTION_STRING=

# The SAP user the logon step types.
# Read from: .env files or the real environment (the sap package; features/steps/fixtures.ts defaults it for the simulator)
# Values: a SAP user name
# Default: unset (the Gherkin features use TESTUSER with the simulator) | Required: yes for a real SAP logon | Secret: no
# Example: SAP_USER=QA_TESTER
# SAP_USER=

# The password of SAP_USER. The sap package can also read it from a protected store (npx sap-gui-secret set SAP_PASSWORD).
# Read from: .env files or the real environment (the sap package; features/steps/fixtures.ts defaults it for the simulator)
# Values: any text (wrap a value containing # $ or quotes in double quotes)
# Default: unset (the Gherkin features use the simulator's made-up value) | Required: yes for a real SAP logon | Secret: YES - .env.<name>.local or a CI secret, never a committed file
# Example (in .env.qa.local): SAP_PASSWORD="<sap-password>"
# SAP_PASSWORD=

# The SAP client (Mandant) typed on the logon screen.
# Read from: .env files or the real environment (the sap package; features/steps/fixtures.ts defaults it to 100 for the simulator)
# Values: a three-digit client number
# Default: unset (the logon screen's own) | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: SAP_CLIENT=100
# SAP_CLIENT=

# The logon language typed on the logon screen.
# Read from: .env files or the real environment (the sap package)
# Values: a SAP language key such as EN, DE, FR
# Default: unset (the logon screen's own) | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: SAP_LANGUAGE=EN
# SAP_LANGUAGE=

# How the bridge to SAP GUI runs. remote = on the remote desktop host through the REMOTE_* keys below (always one worker).
# Read from: .env files or the real environment (the sap package)
# Values: powershell | java | remote
# Default: powershell | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: SAP_GUI_TRANSPORT=remote
# SAP_GUI_TRANSPORT=powershell

# Where saplogon.exe is, so SAP_GUI_MODE=open can start SAP Logon when it is not running.
# Read from: .env files or the real environment (the sap package)
# Values: the full path of saplogon.exe (single-quote a Windows path with spaces)
# Default: C:\Program Files (x86)\SAP\FrontEnd\SAPgui\saplogon.exe | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: SAP_GUI_LOGON_PATH='C:\Program Files (x86)\SAP\FrontEnd\SAPgui\saplogon.exe'
# SAP_GUI_LOGON_PATH=

# The longest any one SAP GUI step waits.
# Read from: .env files or the real environment (the sap package)
# Values: milliseconds, a whole number
# Default: 30000 | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: SAP_GUI_TIMEOUT_MS=60000
# SAP_GUI_TIMEOUT_MS=30000

# How much evidence (step journal, screenshots) the SAP tests attach to the report.
# Read from: .env files or the real environment (the sap package)
# Values: off | failure | steps
# Default: failure | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: SAP_GUI_EVIDENCE=steps
# SAP_GUI_EVIDENCE=failure


# ---- Remote desktop host (utils docs/REMOTE_DESKTOP.md) - NOT VERIFIED on a real Windows host ----------------------------
# Run API, database and SAP steps on a remote Windows host that is reachable only by Remote Desktop (for example from an AWS
# WorkSpace); features/remote-host.feature is the worked example (against a stand-in agent). Read by the utils package through
# remote.remoteConfigFromEnv(), so these work in .env files and in the real environment; with REMOTE_ENABLED unset or false
# every other REMOTE_* key is ignored. Put REMOTE_RDP_PASSWORD and REMOTE_DB_CONNECTION_STRING in .env.<name>.local, never in a
# committed file. The keys documented here are the ones this repo's README uses; all other REMOTE_* keys (REMOTE_RDP_DOMAIN,
# REMOTE_ALLOW, REMOTE_AGENT_START, REMOTE_SHARE_DIR, REMOTE_DRIVE_LETTER, the timeouts ...): see
# https://github.com/achaljoshi/referenced-automation-utils/blob/master/.env.example and utils docs/REMOTE_DESKTOP.md.

# Master switch for the remote host.
# Read from: .env files or the real environment
# Values: true | false (also 1/0, yes/no, on/off)
# Default: false | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: REMOTE_ENABLED=true
# REMOTE_ENABLED=false

# Host name or address of the RDP host (no scheme, path or spaces).
# Read from: .env files or the real environment
# Values: a host name or IP address
# Default: unset | Required: yes when REMOTE_ENABLED=true | Secret: no
# Example: REMOTE_RDP_HOST=rdp-host.example.internal
# REMOTE_RDP_HOST=

# RDP port of the host.
# Read from: .env files or the real environment
# Values: a whole number from 1 to 65535
# Default: 3389 | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: REMOTE_RDP_PORT=3389
# REMOTE_RDP_PORT=3389

# Login for the RDP host. May carry the domain as DOMAIN\user (do not double-quote a value with backslashes, see the TIP above).
# Read from: .env files or the real environment
# Values: a user name, or DOMAIN\user
# Default: unset | Required: yes with REMOTE_AGENT_START=alternate-shell (this machine logs in for you); no for startup / manual | Secret: no
# Example: REMOTE_RDP_USER='EXAMPLE\svc-tests'
# REMOTE_RDP_USER=

# Password for REMOTE_RDP_USER. Handed to mstsc through cmdkey only while it connects.
# Read from: .env files or the real environment
# Values: any text (wrap a value containing # $ or quotes in double quotes)
# Default: unset | Required: yes with REMOTE_AGENT_START=alternate-shell | Secret: YES - .env.<name>.local or a CI secret, never a committed file
# Example (in .env.qa.local): REMOTE_RDP_PASSWORD="<rdp-password>"
# REMOTE_RDP_PASSWORD=

# The .NET DbProviderFactories invariant name used for database steps on the host. The provider must exist on the host.
# Read from: .env files or the real environment
# Values: System.Data.SqlClient | System.Data.Odbc | System.Data.OleDb | another installed provider
# Default: unset | Required: yes for database steps on the host | Secret: no
# Example: REMOTE_DB_PROVIDER=System.Data.SqlClient
# REMOTE_DB_PROVIDER=

# The database connection string AS THE HOST uses it.
# Read from: .env files or the real environment
# Values: a provider-specific connection string (wrap it in double quotes: it contains ; = and often $ or #)
# Default: unset | Required: yes for database steps on the host | Secret: YES - .env.<name>.local or a CI secret, never a committed file
# Example (in .env.qa.local): REMOTE_DB_CONNECTION_STRING="<connection-string-as-the-host-uses-it>"
# REMOTE_DB_CONNECTION_STRING=

# Make the api package's apiClient and apiFor fixtures (and so the Gherkin API steps) call FROM the remote host instead of from this machine.
# Read from: .env files or the real environment (the api package; needs REMOTE_ENABLED=true)
# Values: true | false (also 1/0, yes/no, on/off)
# Default: false | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: API_VIA_REMOTE=true
# API_VIA_REMOTE=false

# The API's base URL AS SEEN FROM THE REMOTE HOST (an internal name this machine cannot resolve).
# Read from: .env files or the real environment (the api package; used with API_VIA_REMOTE=true and the remoteApiClient fixture)
# Values: an absolute URL with scheme, e.g. https://api.internal.example
# Default: unset (then the project's baseURL, then API_BASE_URL) | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: REMOTE_API_BASE_URL=https://api.internal.example
# REMOTE_API_BASE_URL=


# ---- Install scripts and the CI pipeline (scripts/setup.sh, scripts/setup.bat, .gitlab-ci.yml) -------------------------
# These run before any .env file could be read, so they are real-environment variables (GitLab: CI/CD variables).

# The npm registry that scripts/setup.sh|.bat and the CI jobs install from. The scripts run `npm config set registry` with it.
# Read from: the real environment only (the scripts run before any .env file could be read)
# Values: the registry URL of your organisation, e.g. an Artifactory npm repository
# Default: unset (npm keeps the registry it already uses; on the restricted CI network the pipeline needs it) | Required: no for setup, effectively yes in CI | Secret: no
# Example: NPM_REGISTRY_URL=https://artifactory.example.com/artifactory/api/npm/npm-virtual/
# NPM_REGISTRY_URL=

# Install the @automation/* packages from locally built tarballs in ../shared-packages instead of the registry (same as setup --local).
# Read from: the real environment only (scripts/setup.sh and scripts/setup.bat)
# Values: 1 turns it on; anything else is off
# Default: unset (install from the registry) | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: USE_LOCAL_PACKAGES=1
# USE_LOCAL_PACKAGES=

# Skip the Playwright browser download in setup, for a locked-down machine that uses an installed browser: then set BROWSER=msedge or BROWSER=chrome.
# Read from: the real environment only (scripts/setup.sh, scripts/setup.bat and Playwright itself)
# Values: 1 skips the download
# Default: unset (download the browsers) | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: PLAYWRIGHT_SKIP_BROWSER_DOWNLOAD=1
# PLAYWRIGHT_SKIP_BROWSER_DOWNLOAD=

# A mirror of Playwright's browser-binary CDN (an Artifactory generic remote repository) for the CI jobs on the restricted network.
# Read from: the real environment only (.gitlab-ci.yml exports it; Playwright reads it during `npx playwright install`)
# Values: a URL
# Default: unset (Playwright tries its own CDN) | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: PLAYWRIGHT_DOWNLOAD_HOST=https://artifactory.example.com/artifactory/playwright-cdn
# PLAYWRIGHT_DOWNLOAD_HOST=

# Run lint, typecheck, test and security inside the manual `audit` CI job as well (slow). Set it on the pipeline.
# Read from: the real environment only (.gitlab-ci.yml, the audit job)
# Values: any non-empty value turns it on
# Default: unset | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: AUDIT_RUN_GATES=1
# AUDIT_RUN_GATES=


# ---- Set by the machine, not by you ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# CI systems set this (GitLab: CI=true). It raises the retries default, forbids test.only, keeps all test output and turns console colour off.
# Read from: the real environment only
# Values: any non-empty value means "running in CI"
# Default: unset | Required: no (do not set it by hand unless you want the CI behaviour locally) | Secret: no
# Example: CI=1
# CI=

# Playwright sets this for every worker (0, 1, 2, ...); the logger prints it as [w2] and uses it for LOG_FILE_PER_WORKER. Do not set it yourself.
# Read from: the real environment only
# Values: a whole number, set by Playwright
# Default: unset outside a Playwright worker | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: TEST_WORKER_INDEX=2
# TEST_WORKER_INDEX=

# The time zone of the Node process (dates, logs). Node itself reads it, and only from the real environment - a line in a .env file is NOT applied.
# Read from: the real environment only
# Values: an IANA zone name such as UTC or Europe/London
# Default: the machine's own time zone | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: TZ=UTC
# TZ=UTC
